{"subjectId":"sql","id":"numeric-functions","title":"Numeric Functions & Type Casting","group":"Functions & Aggregation","order":21,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Rounding, absolute values, modulo and explicit casts — plus the integer-division and rounding rules that trip people up.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th><th>Example → result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ROUND(x, d)</code></td><td>Round to <code>d</code> decimals (half away from zero in most engines)</td><td><code>ROUND(2.567, 1)</code> → <code>2.6</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CEIL(x)</code> / <code>FLOOR(x)</code></td><td>Round up / down to an integer</td><td><code>CEIL(2.1)</code> → <code>3</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ABS(x)</code></td><td>Absolute value</td><td><code>ABS(-7)</code> → <code>7</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>x % y</code> / <code>MOD(x, y)</code></td><td>Remainder</td><td><code>17 % 5</code> → <code>2</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>POWER(x, y)</code>, <code>SQRT(x)</code></td><td>Powers and roots</td><td><code>POWER(2, 10)</code> → <code>1024</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>SIGN(x)</code></td><td>−1, 0 or 1</td><td><code>SIGN(-3)</code> → <code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CAST(x AS type)</code></td><td>Convert type</td><td><code>CAST('42' AS INTEGER)</code> → <code>42</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT ROUND(2.567, 1)  AS r1,\n       ROUND(2.5)       AS half_up,\n       ROUND(-2.5)      AS half_neg,\n       CEIL(2.1)        AS ceil_,\n       FLOOR(-2.1)      AS floor_,\n       ABS(-7)          AS abs_,\n       17 % 5           AS mod_,\n       POWER(2, 10)     AS pow_,\n       SQRT(144)        AS sqrt_,\n       SIGN(-3)         AS sign_;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>r1  | half_up | half_neg | ceil_ | floor_ | abs_ | mod_ | pow_   | sqrt_ | sign_\n----+---------+----------+-------+--------+------+------+--------+-------+------\n2.6 | 3.0     | -3.0     | 3.0   | -3.0   | 7    | 2    | 1024.0 | 12.0  | -1</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-divide\"></i>Integer division and averages</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT 7 / 2                    AS int_div,\n       7 / 2.0                  AS real_div,\n       CAST(7 AS REAL) / 2      AS cast_div,\n       7 * 1.0 / 2              AS times_one;\n\n-- percentage of employees in Engineering: the classic integer-division bug\nSELECT SUM(dept_id = 1) * 100 / COUNT(*)                  AS wrong_looking,\n       ROUND(SUM(dept_id = 1) * 100.0 / COUNT(*), 1)      AS pct\nFROM employees;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>int_div | real_div | cast_div | times_one\n--------+----------+----------+----------\n3       | 3.5      | 3.5      | 3.5\n\nwrong_looking | pct\n--------------+-----\n40            | 40.0</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Both answers are 40 here only because 4/10 divides evenly; with 4/9 the first would print <code>44</code> instead of <code>44.4</code>. Multiply by <code>100.0</code> (or cast) <strong>before</strong> dividing.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-transform\"></i>CAST and conversions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT CAST('42' AS INTEGER) + 1     AS to_int,\n       CAST(3.99 AS INTEGER)         AS truncates,\n       CAST(150000 AS TEXT) || ' INR' AS to_text,\n       CAST('12abc' AS INTEGER)      AS lenient,\n       '10' + 5                      AS implicit;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>to_int | truncates | to_text    | lenient | implicit\n-------+-----------+------------+---------+---------\n43     | 3         | 150000 INR | 12      | 15</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>SQLite converts leniently (<code>'12abc'</code> → <code>12</code>). PostgreSQL and SQL Server raise an error on a bad cast; use <code>TRY_CAST</code> (SQL Server), <code>SAFE_CAST</code> (BigQuery) or validate first. Relying on implicit conversion (<code>'10' + 5</code>) also hides bugs and can disable index use.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Standard / PostgreSQL / SQL Server</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT CAST(salary AS DECIMAL(10, 2)) FROM employees;     -- standard\nSELECT salary::numeric(10, 2) FROM employees;              -- PostgreSQL shorthand\nSELECT CONVERT(VARCHAR(20), salary) FROM employees;        -- SQL Server\nSELECT TRY_CAST('12abc' AS INT);                           -- SQL Server → NULL instead of error</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Bucketing numbers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT (salary / 25000) * 25000       AS bucket_from,\n       COUNT(*)                        AS employees\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY bucket_from\nORDER BY bucket_from;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>bucket_from | employees\n------------+----------\n50000       | 3\n75000       | 4\n100000      | 2\n150000      | 1</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does ROUND(2.5) return?</summary><div><p><code>3</code> in SQLite, MySQL, PostgreSQL (numeric), SQL Server and Oracle — they round half <em>away from zero</em>. But PostgreSQL's <code>round()</code> on <code>double precision</code> and many programming languages use banker's rounding (half to even, <code>2</code>). Know your types when cents matter.</p></div></details></section>"}